# Testing Rules

The test suite is the source of truth for expected behavior. Every new public
handler / behavior / notification path needs a test in the right directory.

## Rule 1 — Layout

```
tests/
  MediatorLite.Tests/
    SourceGeneration/     # tests that exercise AddGeneratedHandlers() + Mediator
      MediatorTests.cs
      NotificationTests.cs
      PipelineBehaviorTests.cs
      ValidationTests.cs
      TestTypes.cs        # shared request/notification/handler/behavior fixtures
    UnitTests/            # narrow unit tests that do not spin up the mediator
  MediatorLite.Benchmarks/
  MediatorLite.RestApiBenchmarks/
```

New source-gen-dependent fixtures go next to `TestTypes.cs`; do not create
sibling fixture files if a type can live alongside existing ones.

## Rule 2 — xUnit + FluentAssertions

Use `[Fact]` / `[Theory]` and fluent assertions for readability. Do not
introduce NUnit or MSTest.

## Rule 3 — `[MediatorGeneration(Skip = true)]` is obsolete

The attribute is marked `[Obsolete]` and must not appear in new tests. The
v2 generator discovers every handler unconditionally; if you need to exclude
a type from registration, put it in a separate non-production assembly
instead.

```216:225:src/MediatorLite.Abstractions/Abstractions/Attributes.cs
[Obsolete("This attribute is no longer valid with the complete source generator implementation.")]
[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Class, AllowMultiple = false, Inherited = false)]
public sealed class MediatorGenerationAttribute : Attribute
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Gets or sets whether to skip source generation for this type.
    /// Default is false.
    /// </summary>
    public bool Skip { get; set; }
}
```

Warnings are errors (`<TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>`),
so any use of `[MediatorGeneration]` will fail the build in a test project.

## Rule 4 — Use `MediatorLiteRegistration.*Count` as sanity checks

When you add a handler/behavior/validator to `TestTypes.cs`, add a test that
asserts the generated count reflects it. This catches generator regressions
immediately.

```20:31:samples/MediatorLite.Sample.SourceGen/Program.cs
// Show source generator stats
Console.WriteLine($"📊 Source Generator Stats:");
Console.WriteLine($"   Request Handlers discovered: {MediatorLiteRegistration.RequestHandlerCount}");
Console.WriteLine($"   Notification Handlers discovered: {MediatorLiteRegistration.NotificationHandlerCount}");
Console.WriteLine($"   Pipeline Behaviors discovered: {MediatorLiteRegistration.BehaviorCount}");
...
Console.WriteLine($"   Validators discovered: {MediatorLiteRegistration.ValidatorCount}");
```

Tests should read the same four constants directly:
`MediatorLiteRegistration.RequestHandlerCount`, `NotificationHandlerCount`,
`BehaviorCount`, `ValidatorCount`.

## Rule 5 — Coverage expectations

Every new public-ish path gets a test:

- **New behavior** → at least one test covering ordering (place it at a known
  `[BehaviorOrder]` relative to existing behaviors) and at least one covering
  short-circuit if it supports that.
- **New notification strategy combination** → add the notification + its
  handlers to `TestTypes.cs` following the existing fallback-event pattern
  (see `StopOnFirstFallbackEvent`).
- **New validator** → add a positive and a negative case; `ValidationException`
  must contain the expected `ValidationError` property name.

## Rule 6 — Reset static tracking between tests

Fixtures in `TestTypes.cs` use `static` counters/flags for simplicity. Every
test that touches them must call the fixture's `Reset()` before
`Act`/`Assert` or wrap the test in a class fixture that does so. Leaking
state between tests is a concrete flake source in this codebase.
